    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d299">

                <pb facs="0674" n="554" xml:id="pg554"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">511.3 B 1/88</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">President Harding to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-08-02"><hi rendition="#italic">August 2,
                        1922</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: Just a line to
                    acknowledge yours of August 2nd, in which you write me concerning the Arms
                    Traffic Convention of September 10, 1919, which has never been submitted to the
                    Senate for approval. Inasmuch as I have already expressed to you in a personal
                    interview my belief in the utter futility of seeking a ratification at the hands
                    of the Senate I fully approve of the communication addressed to the Secretary
                    General of the League of Nations advising him of the impossibility of this
                    government approving the provisions of the convention.</p>
                <p>Very truly yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Warren G. Harding</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
